1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,338
Welcome to lesson eleven.

2
00:00:02,438 --> 00:00:12,576
So far you've used Deployments, built for interchangeable, stateless apps. Today you'll learn the other workload types: StatefulSets, DaemonSets, Jobs and CronJobs.

3
00:00:13,926 --> 00:00:15,472
Here's how to choose.

4
00:00:15,572 --> 00:00:23,374
Deployments are for stateless replicas. StatefulSets are for apps where each replica needs its own identity and storage, like databases.

5
00:00:23,474 --> 00:00:30,284
DaemonSets run one Pod on every node. Jobs run until they finish. And CronJobs run Jobs on a schedule.

6
00:00:31,634 --> 00:00:35,292
A database cluster needs things a Deployment can't give.

7
00:00:35,392 --> 00:00:43,354
A StatefulSet gives each Pod a stable name, like db zero, db one, db two. A replacement gets the same name.

8
00:00:43,454 --> 00:00:52,360
Each Pod gets a stable DNS name through a headless Service, its own persistent disk from a volume claim template, and Pods are created and deleted in order.

9
00:00:53,710 --> 00:00:55,608
Here's a StatefulSet.

10
00:00:55,708 --> 00:01:00,758
service Name must point to a headless Service, which gives each Pod its own DNS name.

11
00:01:00,858 --> 00:01:08,052
The volume claim templates section creates one claim per Pod, named after the template and the Pod, like data db zero.

12
00:01:09,402 --> 00:01:10,964
A few things to know.

13
00:01:11,064 --> 00:01:18,098
Updates go in reverse order, highest number first. The partition setting lets you update only some Pods, as a canary.

14
00:01:18,198 --> 00:01:23,360
Scaling down or deleting the StatefulSet keeps the volumes, protecting your data.

15
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:35,614
Most important: a StatefulSet doesn't make your app replicate data. Three Pods running plain Postgres are three separate databases. Replication must come from the app or an operator, like CloudNativePG.

16
00:01:36,964 --> 00:01:42,830
A DaemonSet runs one copy on every node. When a node joins, it gets a Pod automatically.

17
00:01:42,930 --> 00:01:48,268
It's used for log collectors, monitoring agents, and networking and storage plugins.

18
00:01:48,368 --> 00:01:55,114
There's no replicas field. And control-plane nodes have a taint, so a DaemonSet needs a matching toleration to run there.

19
00:01:56,464 --> 00:02:00,826
A Job runs Pods until a set number complete successfully, then stops.

20
00:02:00,926 --> 00:02:11,640
completions says how many successes are needed, and parallelism how many run at once. backoff limit sets the retries, active deadline sets a time limit, and the TTL cleans up afterwards.

21
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:18,118
The restart policy must be Never or OnFailure. Always isn't allowed, because the Job could never finish.

22
00:02:19,468 --> 00:02:28,822
Jobs support several patterns: a single task, a fixed number of tasks, indexed tasks where each Pod processes its own shard, and work queues.

23
00:02:28,922 --> 00:02:33,908
In CI pipelines, kubectl wait lets you wait until a Job completes.

24
00:02:35,258 --> 00:02:43,972
A CronJob creates a Job on a schedule, written in five-field cron syntax: minute, hour, day of month, month, and day of week.

25
00:02:44,072 --> 00:02:48,210
Set a time zone, or it uses the controller's, usually UTC.

26
00:02:48,310 --> 00:02:56,432
concurrency Policy Forbid prevents a new run while the previous one is still going. Six-field expressions with seconds are invalid in Kubernetes.

27
00:02:57,782 --> 00:03:04,096
CronJobs have caveats. A run can occasionally happen twice, or be skipped, so make jobs safe to run twice.

28
00:03:04,196 --> 00:03:10,894
You can test a CronJob immediately with kubectl create job dash dash from, and pause it with suspend.

29
00:03:12,244 --> 00:03:21,630
In the lab, you'll watch StatefulSet Pods start in order, reach each Pod by its own DNS name, and see a deleted Pod return with the same name and data.

30
00:03:21,730 --> 00:03:29,212
You'll run a DaemonSet on every node, a parallel indexed Job, a failing Job, and a CronJob that runs every minute.

31
00:03:30,562 --> 00:03:31,740
Let's recap.

32
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:36,794
StatefulSets give stable names, DNS and storage, but not replication.

33
00:03:36,894 --> 00:03:45,576
DaemonSets run one Pod per node. Jobs run to completion with retries and deadlines. CronJobs run them on a schedule, and should be safe to run twice.

34
00:03:45,676 --> 00:03:51,910
That completes level two. Next, level three begins with scheduling: controlling where Pods run.
